.TH SYS_POWER 2
.SH NAME
sys_power \- power off, restart, suspend (SYS_POWER)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

long sys_power(int how, long arg);
long power(int how);            /* through acpid */
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sys_power
is for root only.
.I how
is one of:
.TP
.B POWER_OFF
Stop every other processor and turn the machine off.
.TP
.B POWER_RESTART
The same, then restart it.
.TP
.B POWER_SLEEP
Suspend to RAM (ACPI S3, x86-64): the other processors are stopped, the
machine sleeps, and the call returns once it is awake again.
.TP
.B POWER_SET
For the ACPI server,
.BR acpid (4):
.I arg
is the state (3 or 5), or-ed with the firmware's ACPI sleep types a
(shifted left 8) and b (shifted left 16) from its \e_S3 and \e_S5; the
kernel then uses them to enter those states.
.PP
Nothing is written to the disk first: programs call the library's
.B power
instead, which commits the files
.RB ( fs_sync )
and asks the ACPI server (port "acpi"), which tells the firmware; without
that server it calls
.B sys_power
itself. That is what
.BR poweroff (8),
.BR reboot (8)
and
.BR suspend (8)
do.
.SH RETURN VALUE
0 (after waking up, for
.BR POWER_SLEEP ;
off and restart do not return), or a negative error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EPERM
The caller is not root.
.TP
.B EINVAL
An unknown
.IR how ,
or a
.B POWER_SET
state other than 3 or 5.
.TP
.B EBUSY
A suspend is already under way.
.TP
.B ENOSYS
.B POWER_SLEEP
or
.B POWER_SET
on arm64; or no S3 on this machine.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR poweroff (8),
.BR acpid (4),
.BR sys_time (2)
